WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.055 --> 00:00:03.175
Let's go to...I don't know, the 830

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.295
area code. I don't think we've ever taken a call from 830

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.160
Whose...Who's there at 830

00:00:09.165 --> 00:00:10.165
what's on your mind today?

00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:13.635
"Hi...um...David. I was just wondering

00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:16.505
...um what books would you recommend

00:00:16.505 --> 00:00:19.225
for a fellow political junkie

00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:22.705
um...to like get started

00:00:22.775 --> 00:00:25.885
...like your top 5 books that you would recommend."

00:00:25.885 --> 00:00:28.705
Oh man...that is so tough! I could...I mean...what

00:00:28.705 --> 00:00:31.755
Can you be more specific...what are you looking to learn I guess but

00:00:31.755 --> 00:00:34.565
politics is just such a general thing you know.

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:38.105
"Like...maybe...political philosophy,

00:00:38.105 --> 00:00:40.260
economics...just

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.160
something like that maybe?"

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.295
Oh man...with economics I would recommend starting with

00:00:46.295 --> 00:00:49.315
a lot of the key books from sort of rational,

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.715
traditional economics to understand that

00:00:51.715 --> 00:00:54.525
foundation, and then move on to

00:00:54.525 --> 00:00:57.900
behavioural economics and other economic theory

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.380
so you could start with everything from

00:01:00.385 --> 00:01:03.725
you know, "The Wealth of Nations" (by Adam Smith) to something like

00:01:03.725 --> 00:01:06.635
uh...not that I want to pitch his books but Thomas Sowell's

00:01:06.635 --> 00:01:09.245
book "Basic Economics" which gives us like a standard

00:01:09.245 --> 00:01:12.335
sort of conservative, rational economic approach

00:01:12.335 --> 00:01:15.315
then, pull out something like Richard Wolff

00:01:15.315 --> 00:01:18.125
my former professor and economist

00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:22.000
Richard Wolff's book "Neoclassical v Marxist Economics" 
(Actual title: Economics: Marxian versus Neoclassical [1987])

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.420
To start understanding

00:01:23.425 --> 00:01:26.015
the difference...maybe you would read some Marx.

00:01:26.015 --> 00:01:29.255
uh...I would check out Angus Deaton's recent book

00:01:29.365 --> 00:01:32.195
uh...the name of which escapes me 
(probably "The Great Escape" [2013]) but it's about

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.060
uh...inequality

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.300
fantastic book. I would read Daniel Kahneman's

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.540
"Thinking, Fast and Slow" [2011] and some of Dan Ariely's books

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.080
to understand how real world

00:01:43.085 --> 00:01:46.255
economics is not this hypothetical

00:01:46.255 --> 00:01:47.255
uh...um...

00:01:47.555 --> 00:01:50.575
rational economics. So I don't know, there's six or seven books

00:01:50.575 --> 00:01:51.925
for you to start with I guess.

00:01:52.865 --> 00:01:55.105
"Well...thanks David. I really appreciate all the list."

00:01:55.475 --> 00:01:58.345
Alright. Thank you so much I'll let you get back to...sounds like

00:01:58.345 --> 00:02:01.285
a lot is going on in the background there...so I'll let you get back to that

00:02:01.285 --> 00:02:02.865
seems like a really, really busy day

00:02:03.355 --> 00:02:04.485
uh...over there!

